      <h2>Why Kirkby Housing Has Its Own Plumbing Reality</h2>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p>Kirkby was built fast in the 1950s. The town was a planned overspill estate to rehouse Liverpool families after slum clearance. Around 60,000 people moved here in a decade. The housing went up at speed.</p>

        <p>That history matters when something fails today. Plumbing was installed to the minimum standard required at the time. Original lead supply pipes are still in service in many homes. Copper heating runs from the 1970s upgrades are reaching end of life.</p>

        <p>The good news is that the housing stock is uniform. A semi-detached on one street usually has the same pipework layout as a semi-detached two roads away. We know where the stopcock will be. We know where the original boiler position was. That saves time on every call-out.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <p>Kirkby sits in Knowsley Borough, not Liverpool City. That matters for some things. The council is Knowsley Council. Bins and drainage queries go to them, not Liverpool. We work with Knowsley Council and Knowsley housing associations regularly.</p>

        <p>The town also has Knowsley Industrial Park on its eastern side. One of the UK's largest industrial estates. We cover the commercial units there as well as residential. Same rates either way.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


